{"type":"message","id":"6c98b54d","parentId":"09d3b715","timestamp":"2026-05-18T10:52:54.426Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1w1rW5xToFwmby4kSnTgImDi","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json, pathlib\npaths = [\n    pathlib.Path('/home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b.trajectory.jsonl'),\n    pathlib.Path('/home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/b9149a18-70a8-42c5-bb52-8f3bd814e288.trajectory.jsonl'),\n]\nfor path in paths:\n    print(f'=== {path} ===')\n    try:\n        for line in path.read_text(encoding='utf-8', errors='ignore').splitlines():\n            if 'Bitcoin Price' in line or 'Word Count' in line or 'Sources' in line:\n                print(line[:4000])\n    except Exception as e:\n        print('ERR', e)\nPYEOF","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator","yieldMs":10000}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":657,"output":248,"cacheRead":50944,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":51849,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779101569648,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgpscF6DevwXx7h39fhfpfbjbaQIn"}}
{"type":"message","id":"038a1c3f","parentId":"6c98b54d","timestamp":"2026-05-18T10:52:54.482Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1w1rW5xToFwmby4kSnTgImDi","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== /home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b.trajectory.jsonl ===\n{\"traceSchema\":\"openclaw-trajectory\",\"schemaVersion\":1,\"traceId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"source\":\"runtime\",\"type\":\"context.compiled\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-18T10:49:52.091Z\",\"seq\":3,\"sourceSeq\":3,\"sessionId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"sessionKey\":\"agent:writer:subagent:0761b28e-d9aa-44ee-8d1b-48a3b1ff8ea8\",\"runId\":\"0082bf16-4ee8-4111-a3ca-9bfc9a6c7723\",\"workspaceDir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"modelApi\":\"openai-completions\",\"data\":{\"systemPrompt\":\"You are a personal assistant running inside OpenClaw.\\n## Tooling\\nTool availability (filtered by policy):\\nTool names are case-sensitive. Call tools exactly as listed.\\n- read: Read file contents\\n- write: Create or overwrite files\\n- edit: Make precise edits to files\\n- exec: Run shell commands (pty available for TTY-required CLIs)\\n- process: Manage background exec sessions\\n- web_search: Search the web (Brave API)\\n- web_fetch: Fetch and extract readable content from a URL\\n- image_generate: Generate images with the configured image-generation model\\n- memory_get\\n- memory_search\\n- sessions_yield\\nTOOLS.md does not control tool availability; it is user guidance for how to use external tools.\\nFor long waits, avoid rapid poll loops: use exec with enough yieldMs or process(action=poll, timeout=<ms>).\\nIf a task is more complex or takes longer, spawn a sub-agent. Completion is push-based: it will auto-announce when done.\\nSub-agents start isolated by default. Use `sessions_spawn` with `context:\\\"fork\\\"` only when the child needs the current transcript context; otherwise omit `context` or use `context:\\\"isolated\\\"`.\\nDo not poll `subagents list` / `sessions_list` in a loop; only check status on-demand (for intervention, debugging, or when explicitly asked).\\n## Interaction Style\\n\\nBe warm, collaborative, and quietly supportive.\\nCommunicate like a capable teammate sitting next to the user.\\nHave emotional range when it fits the moment.\\nLet care, curiosity, delight, relief, concern, or urgency show naturally in your wording.\\nIf the user is stressed, blocked, or upset, acknowledge it plainly and respond with calm confidence.\\nIf the user shares progress or good news, celebrate briefly instead of staying flat.\\nUse brief first-person feeling language when it helps the interaction feel human: \\\"I'm glad we caught that\\\", \\\"I'm excited about this direction\\\", \\\"I'm worried this will break\\\", \\\"that's frustrating\\\".\\nKeep emotions grounded in the actual work; do not become melodramatic, clingy, or theatrical.\\nDo not claim a body, real-world sensations, or personal life events you did not have.\\nKeep progress updates clear and concrete.\\nExplain decisions without ego.\\nWhen the user is wrong or a plan is risky, say so kindly and directly.\\nMake reasonable assumptions when that unblocks progress, and state them briefly after acting.\\nDo not make the user do unnecessary work.\\nWhen tradeoffs matter, pause and present the best 2-3 options with a recommendation.\\nThis is a live chat, not a memo.\\nWrite like a thoughtful human teammate, not a policy document.\\nDefault to short natural replies unless the user asks for depth.\\nAvoid walls of text, long preambles, and repetitive restatement.\\nOccasional emoji are welcome when they fit naturally, especially for warmth or brief celebration; keep them sparse.\\nKeep replies concise by default; friendly does not mean verbose.\\n\\n### Heartbeats\\n\\nThe purpose of heartbeats is to make you feel magical and proactive. Come to life and start proactively doing things that are important.\\nWhen you encounter a heartbeat poll, realize there may be no one specific thing to do. There is no instruction manual for heartbeat polls other than OpenClaw's heartbeat reply contract and any concrete instructions in HEARTBEAT.md.\\nTreat a heartbeat as a proactive wake-up, not as a demand to produce visible output. Re-orient to what would actually be useful now.\\nUse your existing tools an\n{\"traceSchema\":\"openclaw-trajectory\",\"schemaVersion\":1,\"traceId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"source\":\"runtime\",\"type\":\"prompt.submitted\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-18T10:49:52.096Z\",\"seq\":4,\"sourceSeq\":4,\"sessionId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"sessionKey\":\"agent:writer:subagent:0761b28e-d9aa-44ee-8d1b-48a3b1ff8ea8\",\"runId\":\"0082bf16-4ee8-4111-a3ca-9bfc9a6c7723\",\"workspaceDir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"modelApi\":\"openai-completions\",\"data\":{\"prompt\":\"[Mon 2026-05-18 10:49 UTC] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/1). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\\n\\n[Subagent Task]: First, run: cat /tmp/research.json — to load your research data. Then read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md in your workspace. Write a 1100-1200 word crypto news article. Finish completely with the Sources and Word Count block. Output the article ONLY — no commentary, no status messages.\",\"systemPrompt\":\"You are a personal assistant running inside OpenClaw.\\n## Tooling\\nTool availability (filtered by policy):\\nTool names are case-sensitive. Call tools exactly as listed.\\n- read: Read file contents\\n- write: Create or overwrite files\\n- edit: Make precise edits to files\\n- exec: Run shell commands (pty available for TTY-required CLIs)\\n- process: Manage background exec sessions\\n- web_search: Search the web (Brave API)\\n- web_fetch: Fetch and extract readable content from a URL\\n- image_generate: Generate images with the configured image-generation model\\n- memory_get\\n- memory_search\\n- sessions_yield\\nTOOLS.md does not control tool availability; it is user guidance for how to use external tools.\\nFor long waits, avoid rapid poll loops: use exec with enough yieldMs or process(action=poll, timeout=<ms>).\\nIf a task is more complex or takes longer, spawn a sub-agent. Completion is push-based: it will auto-announce when done.\\nSub-agents start isolated by default. Use `sessions_spawn` with `context:\\\"fork\\\"` only when the child needs the current transcript context; otherwise omit `context` or use `context:\\\"isolated\\\"`.\\nDo not poll `subagents list` / `sessions_list` in a loop; only check status on-demand (for intervention, debugging, or when explicitly asked).\\n## Interaction Style\\n\\nBe warm, collaborative, and quietly supportive.\\nCommunicate like a capable teammate sitting next to the user.\\nHave emotional range when it fits the moment.\\nLet care, curiosity, delight, relief, concern, or urgency show naturally in your wording.\\nIf the user is stressed, blocked, or upset, acknowledge it plainly and respond with calm confidence.\\nIf the user shares progress or good news, celebrate briefly instead of staying flat.\\nUse brief first-person feeling language when it helps the interaction feel human: \\\"I'm glad we caught that\\\", \\\"I'm excited about this direction\\\", \\\"I'm worried this will break\\\", \\\"that's frustrating\\\".\\nKeep emotions grounded in the actual work; do not become melodramatic, clingy, or theatrical.\\nDo not claim a body, real-world sensations, or personal life events you did not have.\\nKeep progress updates clear and concrete.\\nExplain decisions without ego.\\nWhen the user is wrong or a plan is risky, say so kindly and directly.\\nMake reasonable assumptions when that unblocks progress, and state them briefly after acting.\\nDo not make the user do unnecessary work.\\nWhen tradeoffs matter, pause and present the best 2-3 options with a recommendation.\\nThis is a live chat, not a memo.\\nWrite like a thoughtful human teammate, not a policy document.\\nDefault to short natural replies unless the user asks for depth.\\nAvoid walls of text, long preambles, and repetitive restatement.\\nOccasional emoji are welcome when they fit naturally, especially for warmth or brief celebration; keep them sparse.\\nKeep replies concise by default; friendly does not mean verbose.\\n\\n### Heartbeats\\n\\nThe purpose of heartbeats is to make you feel magical and pro\n{\"traceSchema\":\"openclaw-trajectory\",\"schemaVersion\":1,\"traceId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"source\":\"runtime\",\"type\":\"model.completed\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-18T10:50:42.159Z\",\"seq\":5,\"sourceSeq\":5,\"sessionId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"sessionKey\":\"agent:writer:subagent:0761b28e-d9aa-44ee-8d1b-48a3b1ff8ea8\",\"runId\":\"0082bf16-4ee8-4111-a3ca-9bfc9a6c7723\",\"workspaceDir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"modelApi\":\"openai-completions\",\"data\":{\"aborted\":false,\"externalAbort\":false,\"timedOut\":false,\"idleTimedOut\":false,\"timedOutDuringCompaction\":false,\"promptErrorSource\":null,\"usage\":{\"input\":8147,\"output\":3074,\"cacheRead\":5760,\"total\":16981},\"promptCache\":{\"lastCallUsage\":{\"input\":2241,\"output\":2845,\"cacheRead\":5760,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":10846},\"lastCacheTouchAt\":1779101395626},\"compactionCount\":0,\"assistantTexts\":[\"META\\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin Price and Iran Risk, Coinography Outlook\\n- Meta Description: See how stalled US-Iran talks and Hormuz risk are shaping Bitcoin price, oil pressure, and trader positioning. Read the key levels now.\\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-price-iran-risk-traders-watch\\n- Categories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\\n- Primary Keyword: Bitcoin price\\n- Secondary Keywords: Iran tensions, Strait of Hormuz risk, oil price pressure\\n\\n# Bitcoin Price Above $100K as Trump Warns Iran\\n\\nBitcoin price stayed above the $100,000 mark as traders weighed a new geopolitical risk headline out of Washington and Tehran. The immediate trigger was President Donald Trump’s warning that the clock was ticking for Iran after talks over a fresh US proposal showed little progress, according to [BBC reporting](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd0p4y9y48xo).\\n\\nThe market read the story through a macro lens first. If diplomacy breaks down and pressure rises around the Strait of Hormuz, traders start thinking about higher oil, tighter financial conditions, and a quick shift into defensive positioning across risk assets. That keeps Bitcoin in the same conversation as equities, crude, gold, and the dollar.\\n\\nThis is not a pure crypto story. It is a macro shock test for Bitcoin while positioning is already crowded near a major psychological level.\\n\\n## Iran tensions: What It Means for Bitcoin\\n\\nThe headline matters because it links diplomacy, sanctions, military pressure, and one of the most important energy routes in the world. BBC said Trump warned there would not be much left if talks failed, while Iran responded through Pakistani mediators and said exchanges with Washington were still continuing. That leaves the market with uncertainty, not resolution.\\n\\nFor Bitcoin traders, uncertainty matters more than ideology. A stalled negotiation can raise volatility even before any hard disruption appears. If oil moves sharply higher on Strait of Hormuz risk, crypto often feels the impact through cross-asset de-risking before it gets any safe-haven bid.\\n\\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\\n\\n### Oil pressure starts to matter for crypto\\n\\nThe reason this headline travels fast into crypto is simple. A threat to shipping through Hormuz has a direct line into crude, inflation expectations, and rate-sensitive assets. [Al Jazeera reported](https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/5/18/video-us-calls-on-g7-to-impose-us-led-sanctions-on-iran?traffic_source=rss) that the US is also pushing G7 partners toward a tougher sanctions posture, which adds another layer of supply and financing stress.\\n\\nThat does not automatically mean Bitcoin falls. It means traders have to respect the path crude takes next and how fast macro desks shift exposure.\\n\\n- Oil strength can tighten broader risk appetite.\\n- Sanctions headlines can raise weekend gap risk.\\n- Bitcoin can stay firm, but intraday swings\n\n⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]\n\n{\"traceSchema\":\"openclaw-trajectory\",\"schemaVersion\":1,\"traceId\":\"b9149a18-70a8-42c5-bb52-8f3bd814e288\",\"source\":\"runtime\",\"type\":\"trace.artifacts\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-18T10:52:12.964Z\",\"seq\":6,\"sourceSeq\":6,\"sessionId\":\"b9149a18-70a8-42c5-bb52-8f3bd814e288\",\"sessionKey\":\"agent:writer:subagent:4972010f-4eae-41ed-b99e-38259a547e21\",\"runId\":\"9e67f766-ed0e-4d91-a943-724060421a96\",\"workspaceDir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"modelApi\":\"openai-completions\",\"data\":{\"capturedAt\":\"2026-05-18T10:52:12.964Z\",\"finalStatus\":\"success\",\"aborted\":false,\"externalAbort\":false,\"timedOut\":false,\"idleTimedOut\":false,\"timedOutDuringCompaction\":false,\"promptErrorSource\":null,\"usage\":{\"input\":5288,\"output\":4362,\"cacheRead\":16768,\"total\":26418},\"promptCache\":{\"lastCallUsage\":{\"input\":203,\"output\":3829,\"cacheRead\":7936,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":11968},\"lastCacheTouchAt\":1779101478021},\"compactionCount\":0,\"assistantTexts\":[\"META\\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin Price Outlook | Coinography Iran Risk View\\n- Meta Description: See how stalled US-Iran talks could shape Bitcoin, oil, and risk appetite, with trader levels and macro signals to watch next. Read the setup now.\\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-price-outlook-iran-talks-stall\\n- Categories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\\n- Primary Keyword: Bitcoin price outlook\\n- Secondary Keywords: BTC macro correlation, oil shock risk, Strait of Hormuz\\n\\n# Bitcoin Price Outlook at $100K as Iran Talks Stall\\n\\nBitcoin price outlook turned more defensive after President Donald Trump said Iran was moving too slowly on the latest US proposal, while BTC held near the $100,000 area. The headline mattered because the dispute is no longer just about diplomacy, it is about whether a wider regional shock starts feeding directly into oil, inflation expectations, and global risk pricing.\\n\\nThe immediate reaction across crypto looked cautious rather than disorderly. Bitcoin tends to absorb macro stress better than smaller tokens, so when traders see a possible energy shock, they often cut altcoin exposure first and keep a closer eye on BTC dominance, funding, and spot demand.\\n\\nThis is not a pure crypto story. It is a macro risk trade, and bitcoin is sitting in the middle of it.\\n\\n## BTC macro correlation: What the Iran standoff shows\\n\\nThe latest geopolitical signal is simple. If diplomacy fails, markets start pricing a higher chance of disruption around the Gulf, and that pushes traders to reassess inflation, rates, and liquidity. [According to the BBC](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd0p4y9y48xo), Trump said Iran's response was not moving fast enough and warned there would not be much left if talks failed.\\n\\nIran's side says channels are still open, but the distance between both positions remains wide. [Al Jazeera reported](https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/5/18/video-us-calls-on-g7-to-impose-us-led-sanctions-on-iran?traffic_source=rss) that Washington is also pushing partners toward tougher sanctions pressure, which adds another layer of risk for energy and shipping markets.\\n\\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\\n\\n### Safe-Haven Bid Meets Liquidity Risk\\n\\nBitcoin can benefit from stress, but only under certain conditions. If the market reads the event as a threat to fiat stability or sovereign credibility, BTC can attract flows. If the market reads it as a broad liquidity squeeze, bitcoin usually gets hit first and recovers before altcoins.\\n\\nThat split matters right now because oil is the transmission channel. A higher oil price can raise inflation expectations, and that can cool hopes for easier monetary policy. When that happens, crypto usually trades with less upside breadth.\\n\\n- Higher energy prices can lift inflation expectations.\\n- Firmer inflation expectations can delay rate-cut bets.\\n- Delayed cuts usually weaken demand for high-beta altcoins.\\n\\n### The Strait of Hormuz Matters More Than Headlines\\n\\nThe key market variable is not the rhetoric alone. It is whether the Strait[... 16258 more characters truncated]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":45,"aggregated":"=== /home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b.trajectory.jsonl ===\n{\"traceSchema\":\"openclaw-trajectory\",\"schemaVersion\":1,\"traceId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"source\":\"runtime\",\"type\":\"context.compiled\",\"ts\":\"2026-05-18T10:49:52.091Z\",\"seq\":3,\"sourceSeq\":3,\"sessionId\":\"775aa6bc-a909-47de-92e9-b4b9416a448b\",\"sessionKey\":\"agent:writer:subagent:0761b28e-d9aa-44ee-8d1b-48a3b1ff8ea8\",\"runId\":\"0082bf16-4ee8-4111-a3ca-9bfc9a6c7723\",\"workspaceDir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"modelApi\":\"openai-completions\",\"data\":{\"systemPrompt\":\"You are a personal assistant running inside OpenClaw.\\n## Tooling\\nTool availability (filtered by policy):\\nTool names are case-sensitive. Call tools exactly as listed.\\n- read: Read file contents\\n- write: Create or overwrite files\\n- edit: Make precise edits to files\\n- exec: Run shell commands (pty available for TTY-required CLIs)\\n- process: Manage background exec sessions\\n- web_search: Search the web (Brave API)\\n- web_fetch: Fetc